"Seeing Fine Detail: Telescopes and Radio Dishes",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Laser through a pinhole; Two point sources; Headlights and the eye; Hubble and two stars; Camera lens; Mystery pinhole; Near the hole: Fresnel zones; A disk: the Poisson spot. This worksheet uses Hubble and two stars.",
"Two equally bright stars, {#} million light years away (as far as the Andromeda galaxy), are seen by a telescope with a perfect mirror. The picture shows the light the mirror focuses, and the graph shows the brightness along the line through both stars.",
"The 3D view draws that light as a landscape: each star’s blur is a glowing peak whose height is its brightness, with the mirror beside it. Move the stars together and watch the two peaks merge into one; the label over the middle says how deep the dip between them is. Flat shows the telescope and the light as a photograph. Drag to turn the 3D view (two fingers on a phone).",
"The mirror’s diameter, the wavelength (color) of the light and the distance between the stars can all be changed.",
"The cursor is the orange line on the graph. It opens at the midpoint between the stars, and its readout gives the brightness there as a fraction of one star’s peak (I/I₀).",
"Angles are in microradians (µrad): {#} µrad is one millionth of a radian, about {#} seconds of arc. The width of a hair seen from {#} m away is about {#} µrad.",
"Show each source alone draws each star’s own pattern as a dashed curve. Blind hides the marks the demo draws for the limit.",
